<Window x:Class="EagleBoards.App.HelpWindow"
        Icon="pack://application:,,,/EagleBoards;component/Assets/EagleBoards.ico"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Help - Eagle Board Scheduler" Width="820" Height="760" WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner">
  <FlowDocumentScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" Background="Transparent">
    <FlowDocument FontFamily="Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI" FontSize="14" PagePadding="32,24" ColumnWidth="10000"
                  Foreground="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" Background="Transparent">
      <FlowDocument.Resources>
        <Style TargetType="Paragraph">
          <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,8" />
          <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="20" />
        </Style>
        <Style x:Key="H" TargetType="Paragraph">
          <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20" />
          <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
          <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,20,0,8" />
        </Style>
        <Style TargetType="Bold">
          <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        </Style>
      </FlowDocument.Resources>

      <Paragraph FontSize="28" FontWeight="SemiBold" Margin="0,0,0,4">Eagle Board Scheduler</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph Foreground="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}">Youth and adults sign in on the check-in website. You run the event from the Event page.</Paragraph>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">Setting up</Paragraph>
      <List MarkerStyle="Decimal">
        <ListItem><Paragraph>Start the scheduler. Choose the data folder, the event date, the adult history and the venue network, then press <Bold>Start</Bold>.
          The first time, Windows may ask whether to allow the scheduler on the network: allow it on private networks, or the check-in stations can't connect.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph>Point each check-in station's browser at the check-in address at the bottom of the window (for example http://192.168.1.23:8080).
          Right-click the address to copy it.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph>Add rooms with <Bold>Add room</Bold>. Each room is for final boards or project reviews. For several project reviews in one room, add one entry per table, like 200A and 200B.
          Select a room to rename it or switch it between final boards and project reviews; a board already there isn't disturbed.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      </List>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">The Event page</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Youth</Bold> on the left lists everyone who has signed in, in three groups, each with its count: <Bold>Waiting</Bold> in sign-in order,
        <Bold>On a board</Bold> by room, and <Bold>Finished</Bold> with the most recent first.
        Sign-in numbers start with P for pre-registered (matched a SignUpGenius sign-up) or W for walk-in; pre-registered youth are listed first.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Rooms</Bold> in the middle show who's in each room and for how long. A room's time turns to caution, then overdue, at the times in Settings. They're prompts, not limits.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Find a person</Bold>, over the rooms, answers "which room are they in?". Type part of a youth's or an adult's name (or a room) and only their room is left showing;
        for someone in no room, it says where they are instead: waiting, not on a board, gone home or finished. Enter opens the board, and Esc shows every room again.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>The pane on the right shows the youth you've selected, or the board in the room you've clicked.</Paragraph>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">Running a board</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Paperwork.</Bold> Check each youth's paperwork as they arrive. If it isn't in order, select them and press <Bold>Postpone</Bold>.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Seat.</Bold> Select a waiting youth. The scheduler proposes a board: a room, a qualified chair, and members from other units. Change any of it:
        choose another room, add or remove members, or pick a different chair. Anything wrong is listed above the <Bold>Seat board</Bold> button as you go.
        When something is allowed but unusual (a member from the youth's own unit, a larger board, a room set up for the other board type), the button says <Bold>Seat anyway</Bold>.
        Seating sends the members to the room to read the paperwork; the youth waits outside.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>Marks after an adult's name: the Wood Badge pentagon for someone counting the event toward a Wood Badge ticket item,
        and a warning triangle for someone from the youth's own unit. Point at a mark to see what it means.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>The proposal has the whole waiting line in mind: it keeps adults who can chair free for the boards still to come, uses adults whose unit rules them out for youth still waiting,
        favours adults who came to serve on any board (not for one particular youth, or counting the event toward a Wood Badge ticket item), and then those who have waited longest.
        If there aren't enough adults for all of that, it still proposes the best board it can.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>Your picks are kept while you click around. <Bold>Start over</Bold> clears them and proposes a fresh board.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Start.</Bold> When the members have finished reading, fetch the youth (and their leader or coach to introduce them; <Bold>Leaders and parents</Bold> shows where they are) and press <Bold>Start review</Bold>.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Complete.</Bold> Choose the result, add any notes, and press <Bold>Complete</Bold>. The room and members are freed, and the pane still shows where the youth's leaders and parents are.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>At adult sign-in</Bold> people can say "No thanks" to one kind of board (they are never seated on it), whether the event counts toward a Wood Badge ticket item,
        and which youth they came to support. Those they came to support are listed under <Bold>Leaders and parents</Bold>, with where to find them.
        If someone came with a youth but didn't say so, select the youth and press <Bold>Link an adult</Bold>; <Bold>Unlink</Bold> undoes it.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Someone has to leave mid-board.</Bold> Select the board (the youth, or their room). Under <Bold>Board members</Bold>, press <Bold>Replace</Bold> next to them and choose who takes their place,
        or remove them, or <Bold>Add member</Bold>. <Bold>Change chair</Bold> hands the chair to another qualified member. The same rules as seating apply, and the board's time isn't reset.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Reset</Bold> undoes a seating: the youth goes back to waiting and the room and members are freed. <Bold>Move</Bold> moves the selected room's board to another room, or swaps two boards.</Paragraph>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">Who can sit on a board</Paragraph>
      <List>
        <ListItem><Paragraph>A board of review has three to six members (Guide to Advancement 8.0.0.3). A project proposal review isn't a board of review; this district runs it with two, up to six.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph>The chair must be someone whose role for that board type is Chair, and must sit on the board. If the qualified chairs are all busy, make someone a chair on the Adults page rather than seating a member as chair.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph>This council doesn't permit adults from the youth's own unit. You can seat them anyway down to the national rule (Guide to Advancement 8.0.3.0), which still needs one member from outside the unit. A board made entirely of the youth's unit can't be seated.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph>Someone already on a board, gone home, or unavailable for that board type can't be seated.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      </List>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">The menu bar</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>View</Bold> switches between the Event page and a page for each table the scheduler keeps. Every table can be corrected where it is:
        select a row and double-click a cell to change it. The change is saved as soon as you leave the cell.</Paragraph>
      <List>
        <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Results</Bold>: every board at the event and its result, to correct a result or its notes. <Bold>Open on Event page</Bold> shows the selected board there;
          <Bold>Save report</Bold> saves the results as a CSV file for Excel. A youth's status, and who sat on their board, change only on the Event page, through its steps.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Adults</Bold>: the adults signed in. Change someone's roles, unit or Wood Badge here; their name, unit, contact and roles change in the adult history too,
          so someone made a chair today is a chair the next time they sign in. Mark someone <Bold>Gone home</Bold> when they leave, and <Bold>Back</Bold> if they return.
          <Bold>Add adult</Bold> signs in someone who would rather not use the tablet: search the adult history to fill the form in.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Youth</Bold>, <Bold>Pre-registered</Bold> and <Bold>Rooms</Bold>: every other record the event keeps, including its sign-ups.
          <Bold>Export</Bold> saves a table as a CSV file; <Bold>Delete</Bold> removes a record for good, after asking.</Paragraph></ListItem>
        <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Adult history CSV</Bold>: every adult who has signed in at any event, as the file keeps them, and the last event each came to. It can't be changed here:
          a sign-in writes it, and a change on Adults reaches it. <Bold>Sign in for today</Bold> puts the selected adult on today's list, as if they had signed in at the tablet.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      </List>
      <Paragraph><Bold>File</Bold> has <Bold>Save report</Bold>, the check-in <Bold>QR code</Bold>, and <Bold>Settings</Bold>, the times at which a room turns to caution and to overdue.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph><Bold>Edit › Undo</Bold> takes back the last board step, room change, gone home or link. <Bold>Help › Donate</Bold> shows the ways to support the scheduler's development.</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>Keyboard: Alt or F10 opens the menu bar. Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+7 open the pages in the View menu's order. Ctrl+F finds a person (on a table page, anything in it). Ctrl+Enter takes the selected youth's next step (Seat board, Start review or Complete). Ctrl+S saves the report, Ctrl+Z undoes, Ctrl+comma opens Settings.
        F5 refreshes. F1 opens this help.</Paragraph>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">Where the data is</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>Each event has its own folder named by date inside the data folder, holding scouts.csv, adults.csv, rooms.csv and scouts_scheduled.csv. The adult history builds up across every event.
        These files hold personal information about minors: keep the data folder on this computer and out of shared or cloud folders.
        The files use the same format as the Java version, so either one can read the other's.</Paragraph>

      <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H}">Pre-registration</Paragraph>
      <Paragraph>With a SignUpGenius API key (a line SUG_KEY=your-key in a file named .env in the data folder, or the SUG_KEY environment variable), the scheduler imports the event's sign-ups at start:
        youth become pre-registrations, which is what makes them P, and adults are added to the adult history by email.</Paragraph>
    </FlowDocument>
  </FlowDocumentScrollViewer>
</Window>
